Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  where's my second case of lacroix  i must have looked baffled as i  delivered 246  worth of groceries to an office manager  on market street in san francisco  i  was fully out of breath  i just climbed up five flights of stairs  carrying a good 40 pounds of groceries  and what i get is not a thank you  but an accusation  where's my damn lacroix  what she didn't know was that as i was  biking over to her office i almost got  into a car accident  the mercedes adjacent to me swerved into  my lane and to dodge him i had to hit  the curb  i was ultimately okay  but the unfortunate victim of that  journey  was that second case of lacroix  had i been seriously injured  i would have been compensated  nothing  isn't it so funny in america if you are  an employee you fully expect your  employers to compensate you in two parts  wages and benefits of which one of the  largest parts of that is healthcare  but if you are a contractor  like i was at that moment  everything goes out the window  my name is joshua and i've dedicated my  career to building geek platforms at  uber and doordash in fact i led the  driver pricing team at uber so how much  workers are compensated is bread and  butter to my everyday  and yet  i hear so many stories filled with  anguish and frustration  from gig workers every day  so to understand the issue better  i signed up to be an instacart shopper  i traded my laptop for a shopping cart  and a bike you can check out my cool  setup right here  and in this role i realized just how  hard the job was  i wasn't paid nearly enough to live in a  tier 1 city like san francisco  i was on the hook for all performance  related expenses  and no surprise i had zero benefits  and this isn't just a problem for an  instacart shopper  or a door dash driver  twenty percent of our economy are gig  workers  that's 57 million americans  tech giants like google have more  contractors on their payroll than  full-time employees  universities are increasingly hiring  more adjunct professors where they're  not on tenure track and therefore don't  actually get more benefits  in california  we have argued back and forth on whether  contractors are employees and vice versa  and i say  prop 22 ab5 all of these bills  are red herring  let's solve the real problem here  let's decouple health care benefits from  employers  let me say this again  let's decouple health care benefits from  employers  [Applause]  right now the way our system is is a  historical accident  one company kaiser steel was astute  enough to realize after world war ii  when the government put in place wage  ceilings because we had an excess of  jobs and a shortage of workers that if  they allowed the use of the on-site  clinic as a benefit that was considered  non-inflationary  they double checked with the irs and the  irs said uh yeah yes that makes sense  and so they did that and it was a really  effective recruiting tool  and so other employers followed  and that's why employers pay for our  healthcare  and look this makes sense  in 1940s when an american stayed in one  job their whole lives  but we're now in the next century  where a worker changes jobs more than  five times before they even turn 30.  this system  no longer makes sense  why should switching jobs from driving  at gm as a factory worker tester to  driving for uber have anything to do  with your health insurance  let me confess  i'm canadian  so growing up i never thought much about  health care i never had surprise bills  a ride to the ambulance didn't cost me  my month's rent  and everything i needed from  medication to surgeries to services  were all covered  and when you have chronic illnesses like  diabetes  life-sustaining drugs like insulin costs  less than twelve dollars a pop versus  north of a hundred dollars here  all the while infant mortality rates are  lower and health expectancy is higher  and just look at how much more the u.s  spends on health care  double  double that of canada's  this past month lawmakers in california  had the rare opportunity to overhaul the  california healthcare system by  providing medical coverage for every  single resident  that bill was killed before it even made  it onto the floor  because critics said that it was  too crazy  and too expensive  well you want to talk expensive let's  talk about the current u.s healthcare  system  every single year employers pay 690  billion dollars to pay for employees  health insurance  while employees still have to pay out of  pocket for co-pays and deductibles and  if you're not in this employer employee  binary so if you're self-employed if  you're a contractor if you're in between  jobs you have to buy from a separate  exchange usually at higher rates because  it's assumed you have more health  defects when you don't just because your  pool is smaller  and on top of all of this the government  subsidizes the so you as the taxpayers  also pay into the current high-cost  low-performing u.s healthcare system  it's scatter shot it's wasteful  and some would even say  it's crazy  the center for medicaid and medicare  found that we could reduce health care  costs by 13  or 600 billion dollars a year if we  decouple health care from employers  why is that  number one it reduces the number of  middlemen and brokers in this process  all making a margin  number two  we reduce the amount of administrative  costs of which right now the us pays  four times that of canada and other  wealthy countries  so what do we do  well there's a lot of really smart  people thinking about this problem so i  don't want to be too prescriptive  but i'll leave you with two models  the first one is the ka model we've  talked about it's a single-payer  universal healthcare system that a lot  of progressives in the us are vying for  but similarly conservatives also want to  decouple the health care system from  employers they just want to create one  large health care exchange  labor laws would say that if your  employer no longer had to pay for your  benefits they need to increase your  wages to stay competitive  and in that world  what happens  everyone buys from one system it's  transparent it's open and it doesn't  matter what your worker classification  is  and that's how the netherlands does  health care  and it's actually how we as americans  think about car insurance  you are all future leaders in fact some  of you are running companies right  now you want to do right by your workers  but you also want to maximize profit  it's a conflict that anyone operating a  business that uses contractors will have  to face until we decouple health care  from employers because doing so will not  only increase job mobility  and reduce the burden on businesses to  think about this but ultimately it'll  spur innovation in america  right now if you have a really great  idea the reason you're not pursuing it  should not be because you're afraid  you're going to lose your health  insurance  the current system is creating friction  to entrepreneurship  we're always afraid of the next bubble  we had the internet bubble in the 2000s  the housing and credit crisis in 2008  we're currently in a student debt crisis  but what happens when a fifth of the u.s  economy has a rainy day  and no safety net to fall back on  imagine yourself or your friends or your  family biking delivering your groceries  and then out of nowhere a car bee lines  you to the curb  one medical bill  one emergency room visit  will push you to the brink of financial  ruin  this is the reality for 57 million  americans  and that number is only increasing  while health outcomes are decreasing  decoupling health care from employers  is not crazy  it's common sense  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

